"The figure is a left-to-right white-background schematic with no title, legend box, or unrelated modules. It shows five observations flowing into a scalar correction and back to the same points.\n\n**Canvas and style**\n- Pure white background, no border or gridlines, roughly 16 cm \u00d7 7 cm.\n- Rounded rectangles (corner radius about 3 pt) with 0.8 pt outlines and very light pastel fills (about 10\u201315% tint of the accent color).\n- Solid connectors are 1.2 pt with filled arrowheads (`-latex`). The reuse path is a 0.8 pt dashed connector.\n- Labels in sans-serif math typesetting (`\\mathsf` for words, `$...$` for symbols). Body text is about 9 pt; the node headers are about 10 pt bold.\n- Palette: data nodes (x, y) in muted blue `#4C78A8`; baseline in neutral gray `#8C8C8C`; residuals in amber `#E08E2B`; scalar correction b in teal `#2A9D8F`, the only filled saturated node; corrected prediction in muted green `#59A14F`; evaluation in dark charcoal `#3A3A3A` outline on white.\n\n**Layout (left to right, three columns)**\n\n*Column 1, data (top-left, blue):*\n- Node D1: \"Observations\" containing a small two-row table: $x$ = (\u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2) and $y = 2x+3$ = (\u22121, 1, 3, 5, 7).\n- A single arrow from D1 splits into two branches: one to the baseline node (in column 2) and one to the residual node (also in column 2, carrying $y$).\n\n*Column 2, computation (middle):*\n- Node B, \"Baseline predictor,\" gray. Text: $\\hat y_0 = 2x$, with values (\u22124, \u22122, 0, 2, 4). Input arrow from $x$ in D1.\n- Node R, \"Residuals,\" amber. Text: $r_i = y_i - 2x_i$, with values (3, 3, 3, 3, 3). Two incoming arrows: one labeled $y$ from D1 (top route), one labeled $2x$ from B (bottom route).\n\n*Column 3, scalar correction (center-right):*\n- Node S, a small teal rounded rectangle, the only saturated-fill node. Text: $b = \\overline{r} = 3$. Arrow from R labeled \"mean over the five observed residuals.\" The arrow should be visibly thicker (1.6 pt) to emphasize the data dependency from observed residuals to the scalar.\n\n*Column 4, corrected prediction and evaluation (right):*\n- Node C, green. Text: $\\hat y = 2x + b$, with values (\u22121, 1, 3, 5, 7). Incoming arrows from B (labeled \"baseline $2x$\") and from S (labeled \"$+\\,b$\").\n- Node E, charcoal outline, \"Evaluation on the same five points.\" Text: \"in-sample check; no held-out data.\" Arrow from C labeled \"compare with $y$.\"\n\n**Reuse of observations**\n- A dashed 0.8 pt connector in charcoal runs from the bottom of D1 along the lower margin of the figure and enters E from below. It is labeled \"same five observations reused\" in small italic text.\n- Keep this dashed path visually distinct from the solid computational arrows so the reuse reads as a separate dependency, not a computational step.\n\n**Dependency summary (all arrows)**\n1. $x \\to B$ (baseline)\n2. $y \\to R$ and $B \\to R$ (residuals)\n3. $R \\to S$ (mean residual, the observed-to-scalar dependency)\n4. $B \\to C$ and $S \\to C$ (corrected prediction)\n5. $C \\to E$ and $y \\to E$ (evaluation against target)\n6. $D1 \\dashrightarrow E$ (dashed reuse of the same five observations)\n\n**Text and symbols**\n- Use only the symbols $x$, $y$, $2x$, $r_i$, $\\bar r$, $b$, $\\hat y_0$, $\\hat y$. No extra variables, error metrics, or axes.\n- No numeric axes, no legend, no figure-level caption text in the image."